WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.880 --> 00:00:05.990
Hey there Fellow Scholars I am Károly Zsolnai-Fehér and this is Two Minute Papers,

00:00:05.990 --> 00:00:10.320
where we learn that research is not
only for experts, it is for everyone.

00:00:10.520 --> 00:00:13.580
Is everything going
fine? I hope you are all doing well and

00:00:13.580 --> 00:00:15.600
you're having a wonderful time.

00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:19.060
In this episode we're going to look at a
time-lapse videos

00:00:19.180 --> 00:00:23.500
Let's say you would like to build a
beautiful time-lapse of a Norwegian glacier.

00:00:23.500 --> 00:00:25.740
The solution sounds quite simple.

00:00:25.740 --> 00:00:30.350
Just mine hundreds of photos from the
Internet and build a time-lapse video from them.

00:00:30.350 --> 00:00:34.520
If we just cut a video from them where
we put them one after each other we will

00:00:34.520 --> 00:00:37.010
see a disturbing flickering effect.

00:00:37.010 --> 00:00:41.220
Why? because the images were taken at a
different time of the day

00:00:41.240 --> 00:00:45.230
so the illumination of the landscape is
looking very different on all of them.

00:00:45.230 --> 00:00:50.240
They are also taken at a different time
of the year and from different viewpoints.

00:00:50.240 --> 00:00:54.880
Moreover, since these images are taken by cameras, different regions of the image

00:00:54.890 --> 00:00:57.579
may be in-focus and out-of-focus.

00:00:57.579 --> 00:01:01.659
The algorithm therefore would have to
somehow equalize all of the differences

00:01:01.659 --> 00:01:06.250
between these images and bring them to a common denominator. This process we call

00:01:06.250 --> 00:01:09.820
regularization and it is a really
difficult problem.

00:01:10.450 --> 00:01:13.930
On the left you can see the flickering
effect from the output of a previous

00:01:13.930 --> 00:01:18.369
algorithm that was already pretty good
at regularization but it still has quite

00:01:18.369 --> 00:01:23.259
a bit of flickering. Here on the right
you see the most recent results from the

00:01:23.259 --> 00:01:27.100
University of Washington and Google,
compared to this previous work.

00:01:27.100 --> 00:01:32.110
The new algorithm is also able to show
us these beautiful rhythmical seasonal

00:01:32.110 --> 00:01:35.110
changes in Lombard St., San Francisco.

00:01:39.940 --> 00:01:44.020
It can also show us how sculptures
change over the years and I feel that

00:01:44.020 --> 00:01:47.830
this example really shows the
possibilities of the algorithm. We can

00:01:47.830 --> 00:01:51.910
observe effects around us that we would
normally not notice in our everyday life

00:01:51.910 --> 00:01:55.380
simply because of the reason that they
happen too slowly.

00:01:55.380 --> 00:01:59.300
And now here's the final time-lapse for the glacier that we were looking for.

00:02:00.020 --> 00:02:04.320
So, building high-quality time-lapse
videos from an arbitrary set of

00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:09.209
photographs is unbelievably difficult
and these guys have just nailed it.

00:02:09.209 --> 00:02:12.660
I'm loving this piece of work! And, what do
you think?

00:02:12.660 --> 00:02:16.590
Did you also like the results? Let me
know in the comments section. And for now,

00:02:16.590 --> 00:02:20.320
thanks for watching and I'll see you
next time!

